format 1
adapter curriculum 1
unit: radioactivity

lesson why-nuclei-decay
title: Why do nuclei decay?
minutes: 5
xp: 50
teaches: u2-spontaneous
teaches: u2-random-single
teaches: u2-parent-daughter
relies_on: u1-stable-unstable
relies_on: u1-unstable-changes

description:
    Some nuclei change all by themselves, and nobody can say when. You will meet the parent and the daughter, and find out why this is a natural process.

cite ref-openstax-chem-21-3
supports: Radioactive decay is the spontaneous change of an unstable parent nuclide into a daughter nuclide.

cite ref-epa-radioactive-decay
supports: A radionuclide is a radioactive (unstable) form of an element.

cite ref-openstax-up3-10-3
supports: The radiation does not change with chemical state, temperature or pressure.

cite ref-wikipedia-radioactive-decay
supports: Decay is random for a single atom; it is impossible to predict when one will decay.

card c01
kind: item
type: item
item: u2-l1-predict-alone
scored: 0

card c06
kind: teach
type: reveal
scored: 0
title: Left alone, it still changes
predicts: c01
figure: simple-stable-unstable
figure_at: 1

body:
    An unstable nucleus can change all by itself, even when nobody does anything to it, because the instability is already inside it. It does not vanish. It turns into a different nucleus and gives off [[radiation|radiation]].

